==Old English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|ang|lufiġean}}

===Etymology===
From {{inh|ang|gmw-pro|*lubōn}}, derived from {{m|*lubu|t=love}}. Equivalent to {{af|ang|lufu|-ian}}. Cognate with {{cog|ofs|luvia}}, {{cog|goh|lubōn}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{ang-IPA|lufian}}

===Verb===
{{ang-verb}}

# to [[love]]
#: {{ux|ang|Ġif þū wille bēon '''ġelufod''', '''lufa'''.|If you want to be '''loved''', '''love'''.}}
#* '''c. 992''', [[w:Ælfric of Eynsham|Ælfric]], [https://books.google.com/books?id=2xobVW92JLcC&pg=PA242 "The Second Sunday After Easter"]
#*: {{quote|ang|''Sē Hǣlend cwæþ be him, "Iċ eom gōd hierde, and iċ oncnāwe mīn sċēap, and hīe oncnāwaþ mē." Þæt is, iċ '''lufiġe''' hīe, and hīe '''lufiaþ''' mē.''|Jesus said about himself, "I'm a good shepherd, and I know my sheep, and they know me." In other words, "I '''love''' them, and they '''love''' me."}}
#* '''c. 990''', ''{{w|Wessex Gospels}}'', [https://archive.org/stream/dahalgangodspelo00thor#page/n19/mode/2up Matthew 6:5]
#*: {{quote|ang|''Þonne ġē ēow ġebidden, ne bēoþ ġē swelċe līċetteras. Þā '''lufiaþ''' þæt hīe ġebidden hīe standende on ġesamnungum and strǣta hyrnum þæt menn hīe ġesēon.''|When you pray, don't be like hypocrites. They '''love''' to pray standing in synagogues and on street corners for people to see them.}}
#* '''c. 990''', ''{{w|Wessex Gospels}}'', [https://archive.org/stream/dahalgangodspelo00thor#page/n61/mode/2up Matthew 23:7]
#*: {{quote|ang|''Hīe '''lufiaþ''' þæt hīe man grēte on strǣtum and þæt menn hīe lārēowas nemnen.''|They '''love''' being greeted on the streets and people calling them teacher.}}

